IDEA 1: Contrastive token-gate attribution — From student logits \(z\), take predicted class \(c\), detached rival weights \(q=\mathrm{softmax}(z_{\neg c})\), and margin \(m=z_c-\sum_{j\ne c}q_jz_j\); request penultimate-block outputs \(H\) (`tokens=[-2]`).  
Compute \(s_i=\sum_d H_{id}\,\partial m/\partial H_{id}\) using `autograd.grad(..., retain_graph=True)`; return the top-\(K\) original patch indices. This estimates each token’s first-order contribution to distinguishing the predicted species, using no labels, teacher cache, or coordinates.  
— basis: Wang & Wang, [“Why Not Other Classes?”](https://papers.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2022/hash/3b7a66b2d1258e892c89f485b8f896e0-Abstract-Conference.html), NeurIPS 2022: their experiments support contrastive explanations’ discriminative power; retaining species-specific evidence is the proposed KD mechanism, still untested and approximate because hidden tokens already mix information.  
— closest prior token-pruning work and difference: [STP](https://openreview.net/pdf?id=YVJ2V8R3ZL) combines gradient–activation saliency with diversity; this uses a label-free species margin and signed gate attribution to select teacher input patches.  
— extra FLOPs: one gradient pass through the final student block and classifier, roughly **0.2–0.5 GFLOPs/image**, plus negligible scoring/top-\(K\); measure in harness.

IDEA 2: CLS-weighted concept coverage — Request block-8 outputs \(H\), including CLS; factorize their signed split \(X=[\max(H,0),\max(-H,0)]\approx UV\) using rank-6 NMF, ten multiplicative updates, and content-based initialization; normalize rows of \(V\) to unit norm while rescaling \(U\).  
Set concept weights \(q_r=U_{\mathrm{CLS},r}/(\sum_tU_{\mathrm{CLS},t}+\epsilon)\), patch memberships \(a_{ir}=U_{ir}/(\max_{j\in\mathrm{patches}}U_{jr}+\epsilon)\), and greedily select \(K\) original patches by marginal gain in \(F(S)=\sum_rq_r\log(1+\sum_{i\in S}a_{ir})\); this reads the student’s concept composition and rewards complementary evidence without coordinates, labels, or teacher cache.  
— inspired by: Collins, Achanta & Süsstrunk, [Deep Feature Factorization for Concept Discovery](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.10206), ECCV, 2018, arXiv:1806.10206.  
— why it transfers: Best fit among retrieved ideas because DFF demonstrates semantic-part discovery and co-segmentation from internal activations; diminishing returns could preserve several bird parts cheaply, although its CNN evidence does not establish this ViT adaptation or CUB KD gains.  
— queries tried: representative subset selection preserve sum gradients coreset training CRAIG; deep feature factorization concept discovery semantic parts nonnegative matrix factorization; experimental design selecting measurements reconstruct signal leverage scores diversity; contrastive explanations why this class rather than that class discriminative features neural network; unsupervised object discovery spectral graph normalized cut deep features foreground background  
— closest prior token-pruning work and difference: [PruneSID](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.09480) uses PCA groups and within-group suppression; this uses overlapping nonnegative explanatory concepts, CLS-derived concept weights, and joint marginal coverage.  
— extra FLOPs: approximately **0.04–0.10 GFLOPs/image** for \(d=192\)–384, including NMF and greedy selection; no additional network pass. Both proposals require paired CUB trials at \(K=12,29,49\): neither currently establishes proximity to Full KD or superiority over maskedkd, rollout, tome, grid, center, and random at the same \(K\).